            <ArticleTitle>Statistical diagnosis of lumbar spine disorders using computerized patient pain drawings.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>383-97</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Discriminant analysis is applied to 250 quantified low back patient pain drawings to study the ability of a computerized statistical method for classifying novel cases into one of five clinically-significant lumbar spine disorders. Tests on independent data were 46.2 percent (%) correct overall. Benign disorder (55.6%), herniated disc (51.7%), and psychogenic (56.3%) pain drawings were more accurately discriminated than the spinal stenosis (32.2%) and underlying disorder cases (35.2%). It is concluded that computerized patient pain drawings provide valid &quot;initial impressions&quot; of lumbar spine disorders. Further research is suggested to better distinguish between herniated disc and spinal stenosis pain descriptions, and for better recognition of serious underlying disorder pain drawings.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
